Instance segmentation is a crucial task in computer vision that aims to simultaneously identify and segment individual objects within images. While existing approaches such as Mask R-CNN have shown promise, they often struggle with accurate boundary detection, especially for complex objects. In this paper, we introduce BorderMask, a novel framework that enhances boundary perception and streamlines instance segmentation. BorderMask comprises three key innovations: the Multiscale Boundary Perception Enhanced Attention (MBPEA) module, which iteratively optimizes multi-scale boundary features; the Cross-modal Link Structure (CMLS), which enables information exchange between detection and segmentation branches; and the Equilibrium Map loss function, which mitigates class imbalance issues. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets including MS COCO, PASCAL VOC 2012, and Cityscapes demonstrate that BorderMask significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving an AP of 44.7% on MS COCO, underscoring its robustness and effectiveness. The code will be available at https://gitee.com/shi-junyong/BorderMask.
Introduction
Instance segmentation is an important task in computer vision that simultaneously identifies and segments individual objects within an image, resulting in separate instances of each object1. Unlike semantic segmentation, which only distinguishes between different object categories, instance segmentation also differentiates between distinct instances of the same category. This distinction makes instance segmentation crucial in applications such as scene understanding, autonomous driving, and medical image analysis2.
Instance segmentation methods are primarily classified into two categories: two-stage and one-stage. Two-stage instance segmentation algorithms enhance the accuracy and robustness of instance segmentation through phased processing and task refinement, making them widely used in various computer vision tasks. Mask R-CNN3 is the most representative algorithm in this category, adding a segmentation branch to Faster R-CNN4 to predict pixel masks for each detected object. One-stage instance segmentation, on the other hand, performs classification and segmentation directly on the image using a unified network structure, eliminating the need for an additional region proposal step. Compared to two-stage instance segmentation, it is more streamlined and efficient, making it highly suitable for a wide range of practical applications. Qureshi et al.5 proposed a multi-stage vision-based vehicle detection and tracking system that integrates image segmentation, a YOLOv5 detector, SURF feature extraction, and Kalman filtering, effectively enhancing the accuracy of detecting and tracking vehicles of varying sizes in complex traffic scenarios. YOLACT6 is a one-stage instance segmentation method that decomposes the instance segmentation task into two parallel sub-tasks: mask generation and mask coefficient prediction. This approach offers significant advantages in processing speed and computational resource efficiency, making it particularly suitable for real-time applications and embedded systems. Additionally, there are many other novel instance segmentation algorithms. For example, ISTR8, which employs the self-attention mechanism and Transformer architecture7, achieves end-to-end instance segmentation. Additionally, novel model structures and techniques, such as Mask2Former9 and SOTR10, have also demonstrated excellent performance in instance segmentation tasks.
In recent years, the rapid development of deep learning algorithms has found widespread application across various fields. LLMFormer11 is the first to introduce large language models (LLMs) into open-vocabulary semantic segmentation by constructing three types of priors—object, attribute, and relation—and designing semantic attention, scaled visual attention, and relational attention modules. This approach significantly enhances the recognition and segmentation capabilities for open-category objects, while breaking the reliance on traditional vision-language pre-training methods. CIVN12 is the first to introduce causal intervention into target-driven visual navigation by modeling confounders through a front-door adjustment strategy. By integrating a causal attention mechanism with deep reinforcement learning, CIVN effectively alleviates the issue of agents getting stuck and significantly enhances generalization to unseen targets and environments. LiteMSNet13 introduces a Multi-scale Dilation Pyramid Module to expand the receptive field and capture multi-scale feature information, effectively addressing data imbalance and gradient saturation issues by integrating cross-entropy and Dice loss functions. ZMNet14 consists of a series of Multi-Level Attention Feature Fusion Modules (MLA-FFMs), designed to fuse multi-level features from top to bottom. BGFNet15 integrates feature extraction in the encoder stage with topological relationship modeling of graph structures, proposing a Graph Structure-Guided Multi-Scale Feature Fusion Module to address this issue. Cheng et al.16 develop a high-quality fully-automatic colorization method that reformulates the colorization problem to enable the direct application of deep learning techniques. By proposing a joint bilateral filtering-based post-processing step, they ensure artifact-free quality and achieve high-quality results. Zhao et al.17 introduce an attention mechanism into both the generator and discriminator models of a generative adversarial network (GAN), which enables the generated images to exhibit obvious cartoon-style features while effectively enhancing the visual quality. Chen et al.18 propose an effective seed update method based on tracking-then-refinement using IIF to compensate for location ambiguities. This strategy is highly effective in handling illumination variances and object deformation. Khan et al.19 proposed a dual-stream framework that enhances indoor scene recognition by integrating global contextual information and local features. Specifically, the global stream captures the overall semantic structure of the scene, while the local stream extracts fine-grained details, effectively distinguishing scenes with similar layouts but different localized objects.
Mask R-CNN, as a classical two-stage instance segmentation algorithm, exhibits excellent detection accuracy and segmentation performance, and has been widely applied in various computer vision tasks. However, it still suffers from notable limitations in boundary modeling, task collaboration between detection and segmentation, and handling class imbalance, which restrict its segmentation accuracy in complex scenarios, weaken the collaborative capability between branches, and reduce its recognition performance for minority classes—ultimately affecting its practical applicability and generalization ability. To address these challenges, we propose a boundary-enhanced and streamlined instance segmentation network, named BorderMask. The Multiscale Boundary Perception Enhanced Attention (MBPEA) in BorderMask progressively optimizes multi-scale boundary features, and the encoder-decoder architecture facilitates deep perception, sampling, and fusion of edge features. The Cross-modal Link Structure (CMLS) integrates features through additive and multiplicative operations, serving as an information exchange bridge between the detection and segmentation branches. Finally, we propose an Equilibrium Map loss function, which effectively alleviates the class imbalance issue in instance segmentation tasks.
Extensive experiments were conducted to evaluate the performance of BorderMask. Ablation studies on MS COCO 201720 reveal the significant contributions of the MBPEA, CMLS, and Equilibrium Map loss function to BorderMask. Comparative experiments on PASCAL VOC201221 and Cityscapes22 further demonstrate that BorderMask achieves superior detection and segmentation results compared to other instance segmentation algorithms.
Our main contributions can be summarized as follows.
* 		1. To address challenges such as blurred object boundaries and partial occlusions in complex scenes, a Multiscale Boundary Perception Enhanced Attention (MBPEA) is proposed. By leveraging an encoder-decoder architecture, it enables deep modeling and multi-scale fusion of boundary features, thereby enhancing the model’s boundary perception capability.
* 		2. To bridge the semantic gap between the detection and segmentation branches, a Cross-modal Link Structure (CMLS) is proposed. It establishes a bidirectional information interaction mechanism through an additive and multiplicative fusion strategy, thereby enhancing cross-task collaboration capabilities.
* 		3. To address performance degradation caused by imbalanced class distributions during training, an Equilibrium Map loss function is designed to adaptively adjust the loss weights of different classes, thereby enhancing the model’s performance across diverse categories.
Related works
Instance segmentation
Instance segmentation not only identifies and classifies each target in an image but also generates precise pixel-level segmentation masks for each instance. From a network structure perspective, instance segmentation algorithms are primarily categorized into two-stage and one-stage methods. Two-stage instance segmentation can be further divided into top-down detection-based methods and bottom-up segmentation-based methods, while one-stage instance segmentation includes categories such as perceptual instance segmentation, mask modeling segmentation, and Transformer-embedded segmentation1. Mask R-CNN3 is a classic example of two-stage instance segmentation. The ROI Align method in Mask R-CNN uses bilinear interpolation to accurately align candidate regions, improving the precision of segmentation masks and reducing quantization errors. However, Mask R-CNN struggles with accurate segmentation of fine objects with complex boundaries, leading to issues such as boundary blurring and missegmentation. PANet23 enhances feature representation by integrating multi-level features and employing both top-down and bottom-up pathways, thus improving instance segmentation accuracy. Nonetheless, PANet requires careful tuning of numerous hyperparameters, as improper adjustments can affect the model’s performance and stability. Mask Scoring R-CNN24 retains the basic architecture of Mask R-CNN and improves segmentation accuracy and robustness by introducing a mask scoring branch that evaluates the quality of predicted masks. However, the addition of the mask scoring branch increases both training and inference time. SimpleMask25 addresses the issues of insufficient segmentation detail and the extensive time and resources required for hyperparameter tuning in Mask R-CNN. Queryinst26 generates instance segmentation masks by interacting query vectors with feature maps, transforming object detection and instance segmentation tasks into query-based problems, which simplifies the processing pipeline but increases model design and implementation complexity. CenterMask27 is a classic example of a single-stage instance segmentation method. It introduces the SAG-Mask branch on top of a single-stage object detector (FCOS), combining center region prediction with segmentation head modules to achieve efficient and accurate instance segmentation. However, CenterMask focuses solely on enhancing the center region of the image and neglects the multi-level feature maps at the boundaries, which can affect the final segmentation results. YOLACT6 decomposes instance masks into a linear combination of prototype masks and mask coefficients, parallelizing the object detection and mask generation processes. However, because the prototype masks are globally shared, masks for different instances may interfere with each other. In dense target scenarios, this interference can impact the accuracy of segmentation results. SOLOv228 transforms the instance segmentation problem into a dense single-layer convolution task, achieving instance segmentation through positional encoding and directly segmenting the image grid, which eliminates the need for bounding box detection and simplifies the process while improving efficiency. However, since SOLOv2 relies on fixed-size grid divisions, its segmentation accuracy may be limited for very small targets. PolarMask29 converts the segmentation problem into a distance prediction problem in polar coordinates, generating segmentation masks based on center points and distance predictions. Nonetheless, PolarMask may produce errors when dealing with complex shapes and irregular boundaries due to the challenges in angle and distance prediction. BlendMask30 enhances FCOS by incorporating feature fusion and mask refinement modules, achieving more efficient and accurate instance segmentation, but this also increases the network’s computational complexity. EmbedMask31 uses embedding vectors to represent target instances and generates segmentation masks through these embeddings, improving segmentation accuracy and simplifying the model’s computational complexity. However, the clustering process of embedding vectors in EmbedMask adds complexity to the algorithm, particularly in handling a large number of instances, where the clustering algorithm’s computational overhead can be significant. ContourFormer32 is a real-time contour-based instance segmentation algorithm that adaptively refines object boundaries through a sub-contour decoupling mechanism with deformable attention and a multi-stage refinement process. Additionally, it incorporates a fine-grained distribution optimization technique to further enhance segmentation accuracy and boundary representation. Hoang et al.33 proposed a novel approach for generalized class discovery in instance segmentation, which integrates instance-wise temperature assignment, class-wise reliability criteria, and a dynamic pseudo-label filtering strategy. This method effectively addresses class imbalance issues and enhances the recognition of novel categories. FovealSeg34 is a gaze-aware instance segmentation method that performs segmentation exclusively on user-attended targets, effectively reducing computational overhead on AR/VR devices.
Boundary instance segmentation
Effectively segmenting image boundary features is also a popular research area, and researchers have made many notable contributions to boundary instance segmentation. BMask R-CNN35 includes a boundary-preserving mask head that leverages object boundary information to improve mask localization accuracy. PointRend36 frames image segmentation as a rendering problem, refining boundaries based on the results of existing segmentation models and post-processing techniques. BPR37 is a simple yet effective post-processing optimization framework that improves boundary segmentation results through boundary block optimization. MaskLab38 extends Faster R-CNN by producing additional outputs for semantic segmentation and instance center directions, and introduces a feature selection module to enhance the model’s attention and response to important locations. BAIS39 introduces a new object mask representation that captures either the overall shape or precise boundaries of objects, and uses this representation to generate general boundary detection boxes that can extend beyond the initial bounding box boundaries. SegFix40 refines segmentation results by using a convolutional network to predict binary masks indicating boundary pixels, then identifying corresponding internal pixels by moving a certain distance along the boundary direction, and reducing segmentation errors by replacing the labels of boundary pixels with those of the corresponding internal pixels. PolyTransform41 leverages a segmentation network to generate instance masks and convert them into a set of polygons, which are then input into a network capable of transforming polygons to better fit the object boundaries. BGF42 designs a boundary feature extraction module to extract boundary information at different network stages to improve the quality of predicted masks. It then inputs Mask R-CNN features and boundary features into a feature fusion module, and uses a global attention module to further integrate the features. JLInst43 is an instance segmentation framework that jointly learns object boundaries and masks, enabling the network to leverage both boundary and mask semantic information to generate more accurate mask predictions. Additionally, it introduces an adaptive Gaussian-weighted binary cross-entropy loss function to focus on uncertain samples. BAISeg44 is a novel weakly supervised instance segmentation method that replaces the traditional displacement field clustering mechanism with instance-aware boundary detection. By introducing a cascade fusion module, mutual attention mechanism, and pixel-level contrastive learning, it effectively enhances the accuracy and continuity of boundary detection. DiffEGG45 is a fully annotation-free panoptic segmentation method that leverages diffusion models to extract instance-aware features and generate precise edge maps, effectively addressing the issues of boundary ambiguity and high annotation costs in conventional UIS and WPS approaches. The proposed RIP technique can be integrated into any segmentation framework, enabling higher-performance segmentation results. BARIS-ERA46 is an efficient underwater instance segmentation method that combines a boundary-aware decoder with an environmental robust adapter, significantly enhancing segmentation performance while substantially reducing the number of trainable parameters. MRCI47 employs a multi-range context-aware strategy to extract richer local features and introduces global knowledge guidance to refine object boundaries. By integrating both detailed local and global contextual information, it effectively enhances the boundary accuracy of segmentation masks. Xv et al.48 proposed a 3D point cloud instance segmentation network that incorporates global shape contour constraints to enhance the discrimination of spatially distributed similar instances. By introducing a Global Shape Attention (GSA) mechanism, the network effectively captures instance contour features. It directly predicts instance masks in an end-to-end manner, reducing the complexity of post-processing and significantly improving the efficiency and stability of instance segmentation.
Semantic segmentation
Semantic segmentation aims to classify each pixel in an image, enabling precise understanding of semantic regions within a scene. In recent years, deep neural network-based methods have achieved remarkable progress in real-world semantic segmentation tasks. Khan et al.49 proposed an encoder-decoder framework based on a dense network for the automatic extraction of building contours from high-resolution satellite imagery. By leveraging multi-scale feature extraction and spatial information restoration, and introducing a hybrid loss function for end-to-end training, the method significantly improves building segmentation accuracy in complex scenes. SAMSR50 is a semantics-guided image super-resolution diffusion model that enhances detail recovery in semantically complex regions by introducing segmentation masks to optimize the noise and sampling strategies. Additionally, the proposed semantic consistency loss further improves pixel-level semantic alignment. ContextFormer51 is an efficient hybrid framework that integrates CNNs and Vision Transformers (ViT) within the semantic segmentation bottleneck layer. It enhances multi-scale modeling and contextual integration capabilities through three key modules: TPEM, Trans-BDC, and FMM. An et al.52 proposed a semantic segmentation algorithm based on Mask2Former, which effectively improves small object recognition and boundary precision by introducing a boundary-enhanced feature bridging module. SCTNet53 introduces a Transformer-based semantic branch and a CFBlock module during the training phase, enabling a single-branch CNN to retain rich semantic information during inference, thereby achieving efficient and real-time semantic segmentation. AFFormer54 significantly reduces the computational complexity of semantic segmentation models while enhancing performance by employing a headless parallel architecture and adaptive frequency filters. SegViTv255 achieves a lightweight yet efficient decoder design through the innovative ATM module, while the adoption of the Shrunk + + architecture significantly reduces the computational cost of the encoder. TransKD56 enables efficient and compact student model training by distilling feature maps and patch embeddings from large Transformer teacher models, significantly reducing computational costs and training time. The core modules, Cross Selective Fusion (CSF) and Patch Embedding Alignment (PEA), effectively facilitate cross-stage knowledge transfer and embedding alignment, which are further optimized by the Global-Local Context Mixer (GL-Mixer) and Embedding Assistant (EA) for enhanced embedding distillation. OverLoCK57 innovatively introduces a top-down attention mechanism into a pure convolutional backbone, implementing a “look at the whole first, then focus on details” visual strategy through the synergistic collaboration of Overview-Net and Focus-Net. The novel context-mixing dynamic convolution (ContMix) effectively enhances long-range dependency modeling while preserving local characteristics. ViT-Adapter58 introduces visual task prior information and inductive biases into ViT without modifying its architecture by employing a spatial prior module and feature interaction operations, effectively enhancing its performance on dense prediction tasks such as semantic segmentation. MUSTER59 is an efficient transformer-based decoder that seamlessly integrates with hierarchical encoders and consistently delivers high-quality segmentation results across various encoder architectures. By utilizing multi-head skip attention and innovative upsampling techniques, it enables multi-scale feature fusion and precise object localization.
Although current semantic segmentation methods perform well in recognizing regions at the semantic level, they still have certain limitations compared to instance segmentation. Semantic segmentation differentiates between classes but cannot distinguish different instances within the same class, leading to instance confusion in densely populated scenes. Moreover, semantic segmentation faces challenges in modeling boundary details, often resulting in blurred edges and broken contours. While semantic segmentation exhibits strong abstraction and classification capabilities at the semantic level, it lacks the ability to detect and differentiate individual objects within the same category, limiting its effectiveness in fine-grained object understanding tasks. In contrast, instance segmentation combines the advantages of object detection and pixel-level segmentation, enabling precise recognition and segmentation of individual objects and thus addressing the shortcomings of semantic segmentation in object-level representation.
Our method
Architecture
In BorderMask, the MBPEA’s encoder-decoder structure helps the network deepen its focus on image boundary information. Thanks to its layer-by-layer optimization approach, MBPEA captures global boundary pixel details effectively. Additionally, the CMLS design provides a platform for information sharing between the detection and segmentation tasks. The Equilibrium Map loss function reconstructs training categories through map exponents, effectively mitigating class imbalance issues. Figure 1 illustrates the overall structure of BorderMask.
Multiscale boundary perception enhanced attention
The boundary regions of an image contain rich boundary features, including curves, corners, and intersections, which play a crucial role in object detection, edge segmentation, and foreground-background separation. These features help algorithms better understand and accurately interpret image content. Broadly speaking, exploring additional contextual information in contour areas assists in guiding the network to extract relevant low-level contextual information (such as boundaries and shapes). After extracting edge contour features and performing dense fusion with intermediate feature maps, the approach aggregates the receptive field information of global feature maps, achieving lossless image processing and avoiding any unwanted artifacts. To address these objectives, we propose a Multiscale Boundary Perception Enhanced Attention (MBPEA), with the detailed network structure illustrated in Fig. 2.
Unlike previous works such as those by Canny, the MBPEA employs an encoder-decoder paradigm, allowing for thorough detection and segmentation of image boundary information. Without affecting the internal detection and segmentation results of the image, MBPEA optimizes segmentation boundaries through layer-by-layer multiscale perception, sampling, and fusion, effectively addressing issues related to detail loss.
