In the MBPEA network architecture illustrated in Fig. 2, we systematically design a complete pipeline from boundary perception to segmentation refinement. The entire network is built upon an encoder–decoder framework, effectively integrating multi-scale feature perception, boundary dynamic filtering, and attention mechanisms. In the encoder stage, ResNet-50 is employed as the feature extractor, and Panoptic FPN is utilized to perform initial boundary segmentation. Based on this, a DenseCRF module is introduced to establish global reasoning connections between boundaries and contextual information.
To further enhance the accuracy and robustness of boundary features, we incorporate a BDFM between the encoder and decoder. This module applies Fourier transform-based processing in the frequency domain to refine features and suppress noise interference. In the decoder, a self-attention mechanism from Transformer models is leveraged to fuse and regress multi-scale boundary information. High-precision boundary reconstruction is achieved through pixel-level comparisons and optimization across coordinates.
This design not only strengthens the network’s ability to model fine structural details of images but also enables more precise perception of object shape boundaries, thereby improving both detection and segmentation accuracy.
Cross-modal link structure
Based on observations and insights into different instance segmentation networks, we find that the information flow within a two-stage instance segmentation framework comprises two main pathways—detection and segmentation. The bounding box detection results and pixel segmentation results can be mutually referenced and shared to some extent. In this paper, we design a Cross-modal Link Structure (CMLS) aimed at establishing a platform for information exchange between the detection and segmentation branches. Specifically, CMLS connects the bounding box feature grids from the detection branch with all feature layers from the segmentation branch, allowing useful information between these feature layers to be calibrated and propagated to subsequent sub-networks, thereby improving the prediction results of bounding boxes and masks. Accordingly, the hierarchical features in CMLS increase progressively with the resolution and channel number of the output features.
CMLS restructures and merges the feature information from both the detection branch  and the segmentation branch , and then performs “addition and multiplication” feature fusion. After obtaining a set of fused information, it is separately fed back into the detection and segmentation sub-tasks and propagated forward to the next level of CMLS fusion operations. By leveraging this cross-modal processing capability of common features, CMLS provides more significant feature learning for both bounding box detection and pixel segmentation, facilitating complementary target features between the two tasks. In Eqs. (30) and (31), we present the expression of the CMLS processing flow.
