The Cross-modal Link Structure, as illustrated in Fig. 3, is designed to establish an efficient information interaction mechanism between the detection and segmentation branches, aiming to bridge the gap in feature representations across modalities. This structure aligns and integrates the bounding box feature grids from the detection branch with the hierarchical semantic features from the segmentation branch, enabling effective cross-modal feature sharing. The fused features are simultaneously fed back into their respective branches to enhance the accuracy of bounding box regression and mask prediction, while also being propagated forward to the next-level CMLS module, forming a bidirectional, cross-level information flow. Furthermore, CMLS possesses a multi-scale feature fusion capability, which progressively aligns features across layers, thereby strengthening the model’s ability to represent object boundaries and handle occlusions and overlapping regions.
In instance segmentation tasks involving occluded or overlapping objects, the CMLS establishes an efficient feature fusion mechanism between the detection and segmentation branches, significantly enhancing the model’s capability in modeling object boundaries and semantic structures. Specifically, CMLS employs an additive and multiplicative fusion strategy to progressively integrate the bounding box features from the detection branch, which possess strong localization capability, with the fine-grained pixel-level semantic features from the segmentation branch. This bidirectional information flow effectively bridges the semantic gap between the two branches. Additionally, CMLS performs feature alignment and sharing across multiple hierarchical levels, facilitating the integration of complementary information at different scales and improving the model’s ability to distinguish complex occlusions and overlapping regions. Ultimately, the fused features are propagated through the global path, providing more robust semantic guidance to subsequent sub-networks, thereby significantly improving the accuracy of both bounding box regression and mask prediction.
Equilibrium map loss function
The loss function of the instance segmentation algorithm is obtained by weighted summation of the loss functions from multiple branches, such as classification, detection, and segmentation. The imbalance in the distribution of image target categories often leads to low precision in target segmentation. To effectively address the class imbalance issue in instance segmentation tasks, we propose an Equilibrium Map Loss Function. This function adjusts the proportion of target categories within each branch task so that the BorderMask algorithm can fairly detect all targets. Specifically, in the Equilibrium Map Loss Function, we introduce a mapping exponent to redistribute category proportions. This approach maintains the existing focus on easily classified categories while providing more attention to hard-to-classify categories. Additionally, the Equilibrium Map Loss Function can regulate the gradient fluctuations during the backpropagation process, balancing precision and recall during training.
