To effectively address the class imbalance issue in instance segmentation tasks, the proposed EM Loss introduces a map index mechanism that dynamically adjusts the class-wise loss contributions across different sub-tasks, including classification, detection, and segmentation. In practice, EM Loss first aggregates prediction and label statistics for each category, and then adaptively assigns map weights based on category frequency—preserving the attention on frequent classes while amplifying the gradient weights for rare or hard-to-classify categories. This strategy significantly enhances the model’s learning capability for long-tailed or underrepresented classes, mitigating the bias caused by imbalanced data distributions. Moreover, EM Loss incorporates a weighted balancing scheme among classification, bounding box regression, and mask prediction losses, which stabilizes the gradient magnitudes during backpropagation and alleviates gradient oscillation induced by class imbalance, thereby improving the overall robustness and convergence efficiency of the network.
The “category proportion redistribution” in the Equilibrium Map loss function essentially reallocates the contribution of each category to the losses of different tasks through the mapping index. As described in Eq. (33), the map index is computed by performing weighted normalization on the element-wise multiplication of the predicted values  and the ground truth labels  for each category across classification, detection, and segmentation tasks. This process can be interpreted as follows: if a certain category exhibits low prediction accuracy across multiple tasks —i.e., poor alignment between  and —its corresponding map index will be relatively higher, thereby assigning a greater weight to that category in the loss function and prompting the model to focus more on its optimization. In contrast, for high-frequency or easily distinguishable categories, the map index tends to be lower, which helps prevent the model from overfitting to them.
Moreover, the introduction of the hyperparameters α, β, and γ provides a tunable weighting mechanism for the three sub-tasks in the final computation of the mapping index. This design enables the category proportion redistribution to be not only data-driven but also controllable, thereby enhancing its adaptability to varying data distributions. Consequently, the category redistribution mechanism of the EM Loss can be interpreted as a form of “dynamic attention mechanism,” which adjusts the contribution of each loss component proportionally. This guides the model to allocate learning resources more equitably across all target categories, particularly those underrepresented or easily overlooked, thereby improving overall segmentation performance and generalization capability.
As illustrated in Algorithm 1, to provide a more intuitive understanding of how EM Loss redistributes category proportions, we present the computation process in the form of pseudocode. First, EM Loss separately calculates the basic losses for classification, bounding box regression, and mask prediction to ensure the fundamental optimization objectives of each sub-task. Subsequently, mapping indices are dynamically generated based on the alignment between predictions and ground truth labels across different tasks, allowing for weighted adjustment of category-level loss contributions. By incorporating predefined weighting coefficients α, β, and γ to balance the influence among tasks, and introducing a small constant ε to ensure numerical stability, the method further prevents any single task or category from dominating the training process. Finally, the basic losses are combined using the computed mapping indices to compensate for gradients associated with infrequent categories and hard-to-classify samples, effectively enhancing the model’s ability to adapt to long-tailed distributions and improving the overall robustness of the training process.
In practical training, the weighting coefficients α, β, and γ, as well as the small constant ε, are typically tuned based on the relative importance of each task, differences in loss scale, and empirical experience. The small constant ε is introduced to prevent the normalization denominator from becoming zero, with a commonly used value of . If the task has a relatively small scale or numerical instability tends to occur during the early stages of training, a value of  may be chosen. If training is stable, ε can be further reduced accordingly.
Compared to other instance segmentation loss functions, the Equilibrium Map loss function offers significant advantages by introducing a map index mechanism that dynamically adjusts the contribution of each class to the overall loss across multi-task learning objectives such as classification, detection, and segmentation. This approach effectively alleviates performance degradation caused by class imbalance. The mechanism amplifies the gradient weights for long-tailed or hard-to-classify categories based on the degree of alignment between predictions and ground-truth labels, while suppressing excessive attention to frequent classes, thereby preventing model bias or overfitting. Additionally, EM Loss incorporates tunable task-specific weighting parameters and numerical stabilization terms to ensure gradient stability and robustness during training. As a result, it demonstrates superior generalization capability and convergence efficiency when handling complex data distributions, outperforming conventional loss functions in instance segmentation tasks.
