BorderMask for boundary segmentation in natural scenes
In natural scenes, object shapes often exhibit high irregularity accompanied by complex texture variations, illumination interference, occlusions, and cluttered backgrounds, posing significant challenges for Mask R-CNN in boundary modeling and localization. To address these issues, BorderMask proposes Multiscale Boundary Perception Enhanced Attention in its network architecture to improve the model’s ability to perceive and model irregular boundaries. MBPEA, based on an encoder-decoder structure, progressively extracts and fuses boundary features at multiple scales, enhancing the model’s boundary discrimination capability within multiscale contexts and effectively improving the response accuracy to fine-grained boundaries. This design is particularly suitable for targets with blurred boundaries or drastic shape variations in complex natural scenes. Furthermore, BorderMask proposes a Cross-modal Link Structure to establish efficient information interaction channels between the detection and segmentation branches. This structure employs additive and multiplicative fusion operations to strengthen semantic consistency and feature synergy between different task subnetworks, thereby improving segmentation consistency in boundary regions while maintaining localization accuracy. This mechanism demonstrates strong adaptability to irregular target shapes and ambiguous edge transitions in natural scenes. Lastly, to further enhance performance in multi-class complex object scenarios, BorderMask designs an Equilibrium Map loss function that dynamically adjusts loss weights among different categories, alleviating class imbalance issues during training. This not only enhances the model’s segmentation capability for boundary-scarce classes but also improves the overall robustness and generalization of the instance segmentation task.
In summary, BorderMask enhances boundary modeling accuracy through the proposed MBPEA, strengthens the synergy between detection and segmentation tasks using the CMLS, and effectively addresses class imbalance with the Equilibrium Map loss. This forms a systematic and efficient instance segmentation solution for natural scenes, capable of effectively tackling the challenge of “difficult-to-define clear segmentation boundaries” in natural images.
Experiments
To better understand the significance of the three proposed components, we conduct ablation experiments on the widely used MS COCO 201720 dataset with BorderMask and compare it with other mainstream instance segmentation algorithms to demonstrate the effectiveness of BorderMask in boundary segmentation. Additionally, we perform extensive comparative experiments on the PASCAL VOC 201221 and Cityscapes20 datasets to further validate the superiority of BorderMask.
Datasets
MS COCO 2017 is divided into 118k training images, 5k validation images, and 40k test images. Each image is manually annotated with object instances to ensure data quality and accuracy. The dataset includes 80 object categories with instance-level annotations. The images are sourced from the real world, featuring rich background information and occlusions, which effectively assess and improve the model’s generalization capability. We train the model on the training set and conduct ablation experiments on BorderMask using the test set, comparing it with current mainstream methods.
PASCAL VOC 2012 is widely used for object detection and image segmentation tasks. It contains 11,530 images and 27,450 annotated object instances, covering a total of 20 common object categories, which are primarily grouped into four major classes: animals, vehicles, people, and indoor objects. These images and object instances are sourced from diverse real-world scenarios, exhibiting high variability and complexity. We use all the images to train our model and then evaluate and compare it using only the 5,732 validation images.
Cityscapes feature high-quality pixel-level instance segmentation annotations and comprises 9 object categories, including 2,975 training images, 500 validation images, and 1,525 test images, with a resolution of 2048 × 1024, ensuring rich image details and precise annotations. The dataset covers various cities and multiple scenarios, including different weather, lighting, and traffic conditions, effectively evaluating the model’s generalization ability in real-world environments. We train our model on the training set, evaluate and compare its performance on the validation set, and perform visualization experiments for BorderMask on the test set.
Metrics & implementation details
Metrics
AP is a metric used to evaluate the detection and segmentation performance of a model at a specific Intersection over Union (IoU) threshold. It is calculated by measuring the area under the Precision-Recall curve and reflects the average precision across different levels of recall. In evaluating instance segmentation results, we use standard COCO format evaluation metrics, including AP (IoU = 0.5:0.95), AP50 (IoU = 0.5), AP75 (IoU = 0.75), APS (AP for small instances), APM (AP for medium instances), and APL (AP for large instances). Params (M) refers to the number of parameters in the model.
