Multiscale boundary perception enhanced attention
As described in Sect. 3.2, MBPEA employs an encoder-decoder paradigm to enhance the detection and segmentation results of image boundary information at multiple scales. First, during the encoder phase, MBPEA uses ResNet-50 as the feature extractor, Panoptic FPN as the initial segmentation information generator, and DenseCRF as the inference segmentation filter, progressively fusing input feature information from shallow to deep. Next, at the interface between the encoder and decoder, we design a Boundary Dynamic Filter Module that performs time-domain-frequency-domain-time-domain operations on the encoder’s output to achieve deep purification. Finally, in the decoder phase, MBPEA uses a Transformer structure to iteratively optimize and regress the multi-scale boundary feature information processed by the encoder and BDFM, resulting in an enhanced boundary feature map. As shown in Table 1, when MBPEA is added alone, AP, AP50, and AP75 reach 40.2%, 61.7%, and 43.9%, respectively, which represents an increase of 2.7%, 2.4%, and 3.7% compared to the Mask R-CNN with ResNet-50-FPN as the backbone network.
Cross-modal Link Structure
The purpose of introducing CMLS is to provide a platform for mutual sharing and enhancement between the detection and segmentation branches, where the prediction results of bounding boxes and masks determine the model’s accuracy in detecting and segmenting image instances. As shown in Table 1, when CMLS is added alone, AP, AP50, and AP75 reach 41.9%, 63.2%, and 44.1%, respectively, representing increases of 4.4%, 3.9%, and 3.9% compared to the Mask R-CNN with ResNet-50-FPN as the backbone network.
Equilibrium Map loss function
The problem of class imbalance has long been a challenge in computer vision tasks, and researchers have attempted many solutions. The Equilibrium Map loss function we propose adjusts the proportion of target categories through the mapping index, which helps stabilize the training gradients of BorderMask. As shown in Table 1, when the Equilibrium Map loss function is added alone, AP, AP50, and AP75 reach 40.6%, 62.8%, and 43.0%, respectively, representing increases of 3.1%, 3.5%, and 2.8% compared to the Mask R-CNN with ResNet-50-FPN as the backbone network.
By conducting ablation experiments on MBPEA, CMLS, and Equilibrium Map loss function sequentially, we observe the significant contributions of each component to BorderMask. Furthermore, we also perform pairwise comparison analyses of the three components to further demonstrate their effectiveness. When all three components are included, BorderMask achieves an AP of 44.7%, AP50 of 66.4%, and AP75 of 47.2%. Additionally, the metrics reflecting BorderMask’s performance on small, medium, and large instance detection and segmentation, namely APS, APM, and APL, reach 23.5%, 62.8%, and 77.1%, respectively. This clearly shows that BorderMask outperforms Mask R-CNN by a substantial margin under the same conditions.
